Every recursive function needs two things: a **base case** that stops the recursion, and a **recursive case** that moves closer to it. Miss either one and the calls never stop.

```ts
function factorial(n: number): number {
  if (n <= 1) {
    return 1;
  }
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}
```

Each call gets its own **stack frame** holding its own copy of `n`. Frames pile up until the base case returns, then unwind in reverse. That means recursion depth costs O(n) memory even when the function itself allocates nothing — and if the base case is unreachable, the frames keep stacking until the runtime throws a stack overflow.

A hash table turns a key into an array index by running it through a **hash function**, then stores the entry in that bucket.

```ts
class HashTable<V> {
  private buckets: Array<Array<[string, V]>> =
    Array.from({ length: 16 }, () => []);

  private hash(key: string): number {
    let total = 0;
    for (const char of key) {
      total += char.charCodeAt(0);
    }
    return total % this.buckets.length;
  }

  get(key: string): V | undefined {
    const bucket = this.buckets[this.hash(key)];
    return bucket.find(([k]) => k === key)?.[1];
  }
}
```

Two different keys can land in the same bucket — a **collision** — handled here by *chaining*: each bucket holds a list, and lookup scans it. With keys spread evenly, buckets stay short and `get` is O(1) on average. In the worst case every key collides into one bucket and the scan degrades to O(n), which is why the quality of the hash function matters more than the size of the table.

A binary tree is built from nodes that each hold a value and at most two children.

```ts
class TreeNode {
  value: number;
  left: TreeNode | null = null;
  right: TreeNode | null = null;
  constructor(value: number) {
    this.value = value;
  }
}

function height(node: TreeNode | null): number {
  if (node === null) {
    return -1;
  }
  return 1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right));
}
```

**Height** is the longest path from a node down to a leaf, counted in *edges*. A single leaf therefore has height 0, and the empty tree is defined as -1 — that convention is exactly what makes the `1 + Math.max(...)` line come out right, since a leaf computes `1 + max(-1, -1) = 0`. Depth is the mirror image: distance counted upward to the root.

A binary search tree adds one invariant to the binary tree: **every value in a node's left subtree is smaller than the node, and every value in its right subtree is larger.**

```ts
function search(node: TreeNode | null, target: number): TreeNode | null {
  if (node === null || node.value === target) {
    return node;
  }
  if (target < node.value) {
    return search(node.left, target);
  }
  return search(node.right, target);
}

function insert(node: TreeNode | null, value: number): TreeNode {
  if (node === null) {
    return new TreeNode(value);
  }
  if (value < node.value) {
    node.left = insert(node.left, value);
  } else {
    node.right = insert(node.right, value);
  }
  return node;
}
```

That invariant lets `search` discard half the remaining tree at every step, giving O(log n) on a balanced tree. The catch: nothing here rebalances. Insert values in already-sorted order and every node becomes a right child — the tree degenerates into a linked list and search drops back to O(n).

All three depth-first orders run the same two recursive calls. The only thing that changes is **where the node's own visit sits relative to them**.

```ts
function inorder(node: TreeNode | null, out: number[] = []): number[] {
  if (node === null) {
    return out;
  }
  inorder(node.left, out);
  out.push(node.value);
  inorder(node.right, out);
  return out;
}
```

- **Preorder** — visit, then left, then right (`out.push` moves to the top).
- **Inorder** — left, visit, right (shown above).
- **Postorder** — left, right, then visit (`out.push` moves to the bottom).

Inorder is the one worth remembering: run it on a binary *search* tree and the values come out in ascending sorted order, because the BST invariant guarantees the whole left subtree is smaller than the node.

Depth-first traversal dives to the bottom of one branch before backtracking. Breadth-first does the opposite: it finishes an entire level before touching the next one.

```ts
function levelOrder(root: TreeNode | null): number[] {
  if (root === null) {
    return [];
  }
  const result: number[] = [];
  const queue: TreeNode[] = [root];
  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const node = queue.shift()!;
    result.push(node.value);
    if (node.left !== null) {
      queue.push(node.left);
    }
    if (node.right !== null) {
      queue.push(node.right);
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

The queue is the whole trick. Nodes come out in exactly the order they went in, so a node's children are always processed after every node already waiting at the current level. Swap that queue for a stack and the same code becomes a depth-first traversal — the data structure, not the logic, is what picks the order.

A binary heap keeps one weak promise: **every parent is smaller than both of its children** (for a min-heap). That is far less order than a BST maintains — siblings are unordered — but it is exactly enough to make the smallest element always sit at the root.

```ts
class MinHeap {
  private items: number[] = [];

  push(value: number): void {
    this.items.push(value);
    let i = this.items.length - 1;
    while (i > 0) {
      const parent = Math.floor((i - 1) / 2);
      if (this.items[parent] <= this.items[i]) {
        break;
      }
      [this.items[parent], this.items[i]] = [this.items[i], this.items[parent]];
      i = parent;
    }
  }
}
```

There are no node objects at all — the tree is stored flat in an array, where the children of index `i` live at `2i + 1` and `2i + 2`, and the parent is at `Math.floor((i - 1) / 2)`. A new value is appended at the end and then **sifted up**, swapping with its parent until the heap property holds again, which takes at most the height of the tree: O(log n).

A graph is a set of vertices plus the edges between them, and there are two standard ways to store one.

```ts
type AdjacencyList = Map<string, string[]>;

function buildGraph(edges: Array<[string, string]>): AdjacencyList {
  const graph: AdjacencyList = new Map();
  for (const [a, b] of edges) {
    if (!graph.has(a)) {
      graph.set(a, []);
    }
    if (!graph.has(b)) {
      graph.set(b, []);
    }
    graph.get(a)!.push(b);
    graph.get(b)!.push(a);
  }
  return graph;
}
```

An **adjacency list** maps each vertex to its neighbours: O(V + E) space, and iterating one vertex's neighbours is proportional to how many it actually has. An **adjacency matrix** is a V×V grid of booleans: O(V²) space regardless of how few edges exist, but answering "is there an edge between these two?" is a single O(1) lookup. Real graphs are usually sparse, so lists win by default.

Note that `buildGraph` pushes each edge in *both* directions — that is what makes this an undirected graph. Push only one direction and you have a directed one.

Tree traversal never needs to ask "have I been here before?" — a tree has no cycles, so you cannot arrive twice. Graphs have no such guarantee, and that single difference is what a `visited` set exists to handle.

```ts
function dfs(
  graph: AdjacencyList,
  start: string,
  visited = new Set<string>(),
): string[] {
  if (visited.has(start)) {
    return [];
  }
  visited.add(start);
  const order = [start];
  for (const neighbour of graph.get(start) ?? []) {
    order.push(...dfs(graph, neighbour, visited));
  }
  return order;
}
```

Remove the `visited` guard and any cycle — even a single undirected edge, since a-b also means b-a — sends the recursion bouncing between two nodes until the stack overflows. Note that `visited` is a *default parameter*, created once by the top-level call and then threaded through every recursive call, so all of them share one set. Make it a local variable instead and each call would get its own empty set, which defeats the entire purpose.

Swap DFS's stack for a queue and you get breadth-first search — but the change is worth far more than a data-structure swap, because BFS gains a property DFS does not have: **the first time it reaches a node, it has done so by a shortest path.**

```ts
function bfs(graph: AdjacencyList, start: string): Map<string, number> {
  const distance = new Map<string, number>([[start, 0]]);
  const queue: string[] = [start];
  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const node = queue.shift()!;
    for (const neighbour of graph.get(node) ?? []) {
      if (distance.has(neighbour)) {
        continue;
      }
      distance.set(neighbour, distance.get(node)! + 1);
      queue.push(neighbour);
    }
  }
  return distance;
}
```

BFS expands in rings: everything one edge away, then everything two edges away, and so on. Since nodes enter the queue in non-decreasing distance order, the first arrival is necessarily the cheapest — which is why BFS solves shortest paths on **unweighted** graphs and DFS does not. Add edge weights and this breaks down; you need Dijkstra's algorithm instead.

Here `distance` doubles as the visited set: a node already holding a distance has been reached, so it is skipped.
